WEBVTT
TITLE Closure And Decision Properties — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A class of languages is judged by what it survives and what it answers.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Closure is about operations. Decidability is about questions.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
The pumping lemma is the tool that proves a language is outside a class.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Syllabus position: Section 6 names this clause in its own words.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
A long enough word in a regular language contains a loop, and the loop can be repeated.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Three conditions: |y| at least 1, |xy| at most p, and every pump stays inside.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
a^n b^n loses at every p from 1 to 8: the only legal y sits among the a's.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
The witness has to be chosen, not searched: a^p b^p is the whole proof.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
The lemma says some p exists. It never says which p.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
If p works then every larger p works, so the useful number is the smallest one.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
At p = 1 this page's test language loses 24 of its 132 members; from p = 2 it loses none.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
A wrong pumping length is not a counterexample to the lemma.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
This page's worked language passes the lemma from p = 2 and is still not regular.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
The intersection with a b* c* is a b^j c^j, which fails the lemma outright.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
Every finite language is regular, and for it the lemma is vacuous.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
To prove a language regular, build a machine. To prove it irregular, fail the lemma or use closure.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
The CFL lemma splits a word into five pieces: u v w x y.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
The conditions: |vwx| at most n, and v and x not both empty.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
a^n b^n c^n loses: the window can cover at most two of the three blocks.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
a^n b^n c^n is the intersection of two context-free languages, which is the next scene.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Two automata: a's divisible by 3, and contains the substring ab.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Union, intersection and difference are one product: 7 states, minimal 5.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
Complement is 3 states: flip the accepting set, after adding a sink if needed.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Concatenation 7 states, star 3, reversal 4 — and all seven agree with their definitions.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Union, concatenation and star are kept: one new start symbol is enough.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Intersection is lost: a^n b^n c^m and a^m b^n c^n meet at a^n b^n c^n.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Complement is lost by one line: closure under complement plus union would give intersection.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
Difference with a regular language is kept, even though difference is not.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
A context-free language intersected with a regular one is context-free. That asymmetry is the tool.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Each nonterminal becomes a family [A,p,q]: the strings A makes that carry p to q.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
The example: equal counts of a and b, restricted to even length. 19 productions.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
The result is a grammar, so it is context-free by construction, not by inspection.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
For a regular language, all six questions have algorithms.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
For a grammar, emptiness and finiteness still have algorithms, on the grammar itself.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Membership is decidable too: CYK or Earley, in time cubic in the length.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Universality, equivalence and ambiguity are the three that fail for grammars.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
First check: which class is each language in, and is the word "always" doing work?

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Second check: is the operation against a regular language, or against the same class?

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Five verified questions sit on this clause, and each turns on one of these two checks.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Do the two checks before the arithmetic, and most wrong options remove themselves.

